    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d993">

                <pb facs="0883" n="876" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg876"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.002/390: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-10-19T11:00:00+08:00">October 19, 1937—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received October 19—6:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>827. My 817, October 16, 1 [<hi rendition="#italic">3</hi>] p.m.<anchor n="95"
                        xml:id="d993-f1"/> The British Chargé d’Affaires informed me yesterday that
                    the Minister for Foreign Affairs told him confidentially October 16 that he
                    himself regarded preservation of the Customs Administration as the paramount
                    issue and that he would be willing to accept the original Japanese proposal for
                    the Tientsin area if that were the best arrangement possible. He said that <pb
                        facs="0884" n="877" xml:id="pg877"/> he would attempt to obtain the
                    concurrence of his colleagues but he anticipated great difficulty.</p>
                <p>Sent to the Department, repeated to Peiping and Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
